    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:35:10Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="journalArticle"><analytic><author><forename>Jonathan</forename><surname>Stutz</surname></author><title level="a">The Writings of Mārūtā of Maipherqat and the Making of Nicaea in Arabic</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/SZYS89D2</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/SZYS89D2</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/SZYS89D2</idno></analytic><monogr><title level="j">Journal of Eastern Christian Studies</title><imprint><date>2019</date></imprint><biblScope unit="vol">71:1-2</biblScope><biblScope unit="pp">1-28</biblScope></monogr><note type="abstract">The writings of Mārūtā, bishop of Maipherqat, on the council of Nicaea enjoyed great success among Eastern Christians and from them made their way to Muslim writers. Several works, such as the Chronicle of Seert, the apologetical work of 'Abd al-Ğabbār, the chronicle of Eutychius of Alexandria, and the Arab canon collections testify to the wide circulation which the canons of Mārūtā, along with the set of legends on Constantine transmitted with them, enjoyed among Eastern Christianity. A careful examination of this material allows us to discern some of the mechanisms involved in the reception and appropriation of Constantine's legacy within the 'House of Islam'.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Stutz, Jonathan. “The Writings of Mārūtā of Maipherqat and the Making of Nicaea in Arabic.” <title level="m">Journal of Eastern Christian Studies</title> 71:1-2 (2019): 1–28.
